.\" The .TH version is restamped from the Makefile's VERSION at install time.
.TH GPUWHO 1 "2026-08-26" "gpuwho 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gpuwho \- who is using the GPUs, when one frees up, and who used how much
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpuwho
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gpuwho wait
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gpuwho report
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gpuwho collect
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gpuwho setup
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpuwho
is a single-binary tool for shared GPU servers. It answers three questions: who
is on the GPUs right now, when a GPU will become free, and who used how much
over a given period.
.PP
The snapshot and
.B wait
commands query the driver on demand and need nothing running in the background.
History is an optional layer on top, combining the driver's accounting mode, a
systemd timer running a one-shot collector, and an append-only event log that
.B report
reads.
.PP
It links against
.BR libnvidia-ml ,
the library behind
.BR nvidia-smi (1),
rather than parsing that command's output, and reads
.I /proc
for the parts NVML does not know about, such as who owns a pid.
.PP
Only
.I compute
contexts are counted. Graphics-only processes \(em Xorg, the compositor,
browsers, the rest of a desktop session \(em are excluded by NVML itself and
never appear.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gpuwho
Snapshot. Per GPU: index, name, used/total memory, instantaneous utilization.
Per process: user, pid, GPU memory, uptime, command.
.TP
.B gpuwho wait
Poll until the selected GPUs are free, print what happened, optionally notify,
and exit. Not a daemon.
.TP
.B gpuwho report
Aggregate per-user usage from the event log over a time window.
.TP
.B gpuwho collect
One collector tick. Run from
.BR gpuwho-collect.timer (8),
not by hand.
.TP
.B gpuwho setup
Enable NVIDIA accounting mode on every GPU (requires root) and print install
guidance.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " DIR"
Where
.I state.json
and the event log live. Default
.IR /var/lib/gpuwho ,
or
.BR $GPUWHO_STATE_DIR .
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore " RULE"
Do not count processes matching
.IR RULE .
Repeatable. See
.BR "IGNORE RULES" .
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore\-file " FILE"
Read rules from
.IR FILE ,
default
.IR /etc/gpuwho/ignore.conf .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ignore
Apply no rules at all; show every compute process.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-mem " SIZE"
Ignore compute processes holding less than
.I SIZE
of GPU memory.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never colorize, even on a TTY.
.B NO_COLOR
is honored as well.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help. After a subcommand, that subcommand's help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH SNAPSHOT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-gpu " N"
Only this GPU index. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
.SH WAIT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-g ", " \-\-gpu " N"
Only this GPU index. Repeatable. With no
.BR \-\-gpu ,
every GPU is selected and all of them must be free.
.TP
.B \-\-any
Satisfied as soon as any selected GPU is free.
.TP
.BI \-\-mem\-free " SIZE"
Also require this much free memory.
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-interval " S"
Seconds between polls. Default 10.
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-timeout " S"
Give up after
.I S
seconds. Default: wait forever.
.TP
.BI \-\-ntfy " TOPIC"
Notify an ntfy topic, or a full ntfy URL.
.TP
.BI \-\-webhook " URL"
POST a JSON body to this URL.
.PP
Notifications exec
.BR curl (1)
directly, so the topic and URL are never passed through a shell.
.SH REPORT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-day ", " \-\-week ", " \-\-month ", " \-\-all
Relative windows: the last 24 hours, 7 days, 30 days, or everything still in
the log. Default
.BR \-\-week .
.TP
.BI \-\-since " WHEN" ", " \-\-until " WHEN"
Explicit window.
.I WHEN
is
.BR now ,
a relative age
.RB ( 7d ", " 12h ", " 30m ", " 2w ),
an absolute
.B YYYY-MM-DD[ HH:MM[:SS]]
in local time, or
.BI @ epoch-seconds .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
.SH IGNORE RULES
A rule is one of:
.TP
.I glob
Matches the command basename or the full command line.
.B sunshine
matches
.IR /usr/bin/sunshine ;
.B *ffmpeg*
matches anything with ffmpeg in its command line.
.TP
.BI cmd: glob
Matches the full command line only.
.TP
.BI user: glob
Matches the owner's name.
.TP
.BI uid: n
Matches the owner's uid exactly.
.PP
Rules apply to both the snapshot and the recorded history. They exist for
processes that genuinely hold a compute context but should not count as jobs on
a particular machine \(em a desktop streaming host, a video encoder, a service
account. They are not needed to exclude Xorg or a desktop session, which never
appear in the first place.
.PP
A process already being tracked is never dropped mid-run by a rule change:
filtering decides whether to
.I start
tracking, so intervals always close cleanly.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; for
.BR wait ,
the condition was met.
.TP
.B 1
Error.
.TP
.B 2
.B wait
timed out.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/gpuwho/ignore.conf
Ignore rules. Ships empty.
.TP
.I /var/lib/gpuwho/state.json
Collector state: open intervals and the last tick time. Rewritten atomically
every tick.
.TP
.I /var/lib/gpuwho/events-YYYY-MM.jsonl
Append-only event log, one file per month. Gzipped months are read
transparently. Retention is file deletion.
.SH NOTES
Workloads launched through the Docker daemon appear as
.B root
on the host and are attributed to root rather than to a person; rootless Podman
does not have this problem. Under CUDA MPS, client processes aggregate under the
MPS server's pid and per-person attribution is lost.
.PP
Two different utilization figures exist and are never mixed: the snapshot shows
.I instantaneous device
utilization, while the report's
.B avg-util
comes from accounting and is the percentage of each process's lifetime during
which kernels were executing.
.PP
The accounting buffer is circular. If many short-lived processes rotate it, some
end records carry no metrics; those intervals still count toward
.B gpu-hours
and
.B jobs
but are excluded from
.B avg-util
and
.BR peak-mem .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nvidia-smi (1),
.BR nvidia-persistenced (1),
.BR systemd.timer (5)
